    "issued_at": "2026-09-09T11:15:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "d1d8fc98-dbc5-4be2-99cc-95efe5061877",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 091115\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n415 AM PDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n*  Above-normal temperatures expected mid-to-late week, hottest day \n   of the week will be Thursday when the Extreme Heat Warning is in \n   effect\n\n*  Breezy south-southwesterly wind gusts expected across the\n   region Friday through the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Early Next Week\n\nAnother stretch of above normal temperatures is expected this week \nacross the Mohave Desert and lower Great Basin as a ridge of high \npressure shifts eastward. A hot airmass is currently in place across \nthe region as 850mb temperature anomalies are already above normal \nfor this time of year, supporting highs at or above 100 in desert \nvalleys and 110s in the CRV and around Death Valley. As the ridge \nstrengthens and retrogrades west, it becomes centered overhead on \nThursday. Increasing subsidence under the 595dm ridge in addition to \nthe hot airmass will promote well above normal to near record high \ntemperatures by Thursday in desert valleys; therefore an Extreme Heat \nWarning is in effect for the daytime hours on Thursday for Death \nValley, the Las Vegas Valley, Pahrump Valley, Moapa Valley, Colorado \nRiver Valley, southern Mohave County, and southeastern San \nBernardino County. Lingering monsoonal moisture will also keep \nnighttime lows elevated as well with lows in the 80s likely in \ndesert valleys, preventing much relief, if any, from the hot \ndaytime temperatures. Still expecting a slow downward trend in \ntemperatures into the weekend as a trough pushes into the west \ncoast, bringing slightly cooler temperatures to the region...yet \nstill above normal for this time of year. Expect temperatures to \ndrop a few degrees Friday along with an additional drop around 2 to \n4 degrees Saturday with temperatures close to normal by early next \nweek. \n\nAs mentioned above, lingering monsoonal moisture still remains in \nplace for portions of the region. Generally dry conditions are \nexpected through the week, but weak moisture return northward on the \nwest side of the ridge Wednesday and Thursday should allow for \nisolated thunderstorms in the Sierra and terrain of the southern \nGreat Basin. Not expecting anything widespread, in fact most \nlocations, especially around Las Vegas, will remain fairly dry. \n\nThe aforementioned trough pushing into the western US will bring \ncooler temperatures but also increasing southwest winds to the \nregion. Guidance continues to show a mid level jet streak developing \noverhead on Saturday at or above 40 kts. Mixing into this area of \nstronger winds may result in widespread gusts mixing down to the \nsurface 30-40 MPH, with over a 50% chance of gusts over 40 MPH in \nthe western Mojave Desert. There could also be some gusty southerly \nwinds impacting Lake Mohave and Lake Havasu this weekend, which \ncould impact boaters with winds and waves. If forecast trends \ncontinue over the next few days, wind headlines may be needed for \nSaturday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Winds \ngenerally follow typical daily directional patterns, including \neasterly to northeasterly winds this afternoon and southwesterly \nwinds this evening. Wind speeds remain than 10 knots throughout the \nforecast period. VFR conditions are expected with FEW to SCT mid and \nhigh clouds filtering through the area. Temperatures exceed 100 \ndegrees between 20Z and 02Z.  \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Winds will generally \nfollow typical daily directional patterns over the next 24 hours, \nwith top speeds around 10 knots or less for most locations. \nNortherly winds pick up in the Colorado River Valley late morning \nand afternoon, with gusts of 20 to 25 knots possible at KIFP. VFR \nconditions are expected with FEW to SCT mid and high clouds \nfiltering through the area. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Moore\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
